WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.133
freely moving

00:00:00.134 --> 00:00:00.506
air?

00:00:01.241 --> 00:00:04.287
Because moving air can
transfer energy by convection.

00:00:04.761 --> 00:00:07.205
Small trapped pockets limit bulk movement.

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:11.341
That is why many insulating materials
contain lots of little spaces

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.019
We warm water near the
bottom of a container.

00:00:14.731 --> 00:00:19.645
Explain why some of the warmer water rises
without using the phrase 'heat rises.'

00:00:20.436 --> 00:00:25.180
The water expands, becomes less dense
than the surrounding water, and rises.

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:28.813
Cooler, denser water
moves down to replace it.

00:00:29.569 --> 00:00:30.261
Beautiful.

00:00:30.992 --> 00:00:34.970
Under these ordinary conditions,
gravity and density differences

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:36.554
create a convection current.

00:00:36.893 --> 00:00:39.079
The fluid carries energy as it moves.

00:00:39.411 --> 00:00:41.394
A fluid is a liquid or a gas.

00:00:42.216 --> 00:00:46.547
So convection could happen in a solid
metal bar if we heated it enough?

00:00:47.238 --> 00:00:49.133
Not while it remains a rigid solid.

00:00:49.447 --> 00:00:52.281
Its particles cannot
circulate as a bulk fluid.

00:00:52.741 --> 00:00:56.014
That answer would make our
imaginary mark wobble dangerously.

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:58.165
Conduction happens there instead.

00:00:58.932 --> 00:01:03.804
"Heat rises" is too vague because it is
the warmer, less dense fluid that rises.

00:01:04.586 --> 00:01:05.361
Exactly.

00:01:05.642 --> 00:01:09.501
Energy can also transfer sideways
or downwards by other mechanisms.

00:01:09.929 --> 00:01:13.372
A room radiator warms nearby
air, which can circulate.

00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:17.073
Despite its name, it also
transfers energy by radiation.

00:01:17.846 --> 00:01:22.015
And a hot air balloon rises because
the warmed air inside is less dense

00:01:22.018 --> 00:01:25.534
than the outside air, with enough
buoyancy to lift the whole balloon.

00:01:26.331 --> 00:01:26.879
Correct.

00:01:27.423 --> 00:01:30.123
A daytime sea breeze is
another useful example.

00:01:30.558 --> 00:01:35.867
Warmer land can heat air above it, air
rises, and cooler sea air moves inland.

00:01:36.251 --> 00:01:40.208
Real weather also depends on
larger-scale winds and other conditions

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:41.814
Here is the big challenge.

00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:44.979
Space between the Sun and
Earth is mostly vacuum.

00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:48.797
How does energy cross it if
conduction and convection need matter?

00:01:49.548 --> 00:01:50.408
Radiation.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.544
Electromagnetic waves can
travel through a vacuum.

00:01:53.916 --> 00:01:56.558
The Sun does not need a
giant invisible air pipe.

00:01:57.349 --> 00:01:58.133
Exactly.

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:02.940
Objects at everyday temperatures emit
mainly infrared thermal radiation.

00:02:03.344 --> 00:02:07.664
Hotter objects, including the Sun,
emit across other wavelengths too.

00:02:08.173 --> 00:02:11.712
Thermal radiation is not
always exclusively infrared.

00:02:12.478 --> 00:02:16.652
All objects emit radiation,
even one that feels cold to us?

00:02:17.397 --> 00:02:17.979
Yes.

00:02:18.275 --> 00:02:19.526
Their temperature matters.

00:02:19.984 --> 00:02:24.661
When two objects exchange radiation, the
hotter one normally loses more energy

00:02:24.663 --> 00:02:26.220
than it receives from the cooler one.

00:02:26.569 --> 00:02:29.041
Again, we care about the net transfer.

00:02:29.859 --> 00:02:34.584
Which surface usually absorbs and
emits infrared more effectively, a dull

00:02:34.592 --> 00:02:37.035
black surface or shiny polished metal?

00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:42.304
In the standard school comparison, dull
black is the better absorber and emitter.

00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:45.577
Polished metal reflects
more and emits less.

00:02:46.002 --> 00:02:49.491
But visible color alone is
not a universal guide to every

00:02:49.491 --> 00:02:51.216
material's infrared behavior.

00:02:52.013 --> 00:02:55.689
So we control the other variables
rather than declaring any black

00:02:55.689 --> 00:02:57.339
object the champion forever.

00:02:58.141 --> 00:02:58.923
Exactly.

00:02:59.294 --> 00:03:03.093
Keep surface area, contents, and
starting temperature comparable.

00:03:03.785 --> 00:03:06.098
Also, distinguish
reflection from emission.

00:03:06.596 --> 00:03:09.885
A reflective surface
redirects incoming radiation.

00:03:10.193 --> 00:03:13.243
Emitting means transferring
energy away as radiation

00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:18.391
Our flask has a vacuum between its walls,
reflective surfaces, and a stopper.

00:03:18.963 --> 00:03:21.685
Match each feature to
the transfer it reduces.

00:03:22.431 --> 00:03:26.187
The vacuum reduces conduction and
convection across the gap because

00:03:26.187 --> 00:03:27.931
there are almost no particles there.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.858
Reflective walls reduce
radiative transfer.

00:03:31.299 --> 00:03:34.331
The stopper limits air movement
and transfer through the opening.

00:03:35.084 --> 00:03:35.774
Excellent.

00:03:36.157 --> 00:03:38.887
Some conduction remains
through supports and the neck.

00:03:39.308 --> 00:03:43.399
Real flasks slow energy transfer
rather than stopping it forever.

00:03:44.278 --> 00:03:49.084
Can the same flask keep a cold drink cold,
or is it only trained for hot chocolate?

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:50.320
Both.

00:03:50.682 --> 00:03:53.967
Insulation slows energy
transfer in either direction.

00:03:54.306 --> 00:03:56.661
It does not create warmth or coldness.

00:03:56.971 --> 00:03:59.311
A coat reduces energy loss from your body.

00:03:59.553 --> 00:04:01.694
It does not generate the energy itself.

00:04:02.384 --> 00:04:04.217
Loft insulation and cavity

00:04:04.218 --> 00:04:05.839
wall insulation trap air.

00:04:06.207 --> 00:04:09.938
Double glazing puts a gap between
panes to reduce transfer too.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:11.291
Correct.

00:04:11.656 --> 00:04:16.680
Ordinary double glazing usually has air
or another gas in that gap, not a vacuum.

00:04:17.101 --> 00:04:20.810
All these designs reduce heating
needs, though ventilation and moisture

00:04:20.813 --> 00:04:22.855
control still matter in a real home.

00:04:23.631 --> 00:04:28.030
Fur and feathers trap air, whereas
blubber is an insulating fat layer.

00:04:28.505 --> 00:04:30.918
Different structures, related purpose.

00:04:31.651 --> 00:04:32.413
Well spotted.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:37.642
In an exam, name the feature, the
transfer mechanism, and why it is reduced.

00:04:38.004 --> 00:04:40.000
Saying it keeps heat in may describe the
outcome without explaining the science
